<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denizli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ladik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denizli is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea on the Lycus</a>. The medieval Turkish town, known as Ladik, grew up nearby after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> groups took the area in the late 13th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it around 1333, praising its gold-embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth. Today Denizli is a provincial capital and a centre of the Turkish textile industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denizli.jpg" alt="Denizli" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karelj · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pamukale_Turkey_2013_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
